WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.880
Neutron-star mergers amplify magnetic fields more than 300 times in one millisecond, reshaping the remnant. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 229, a cosmic deep dive on turbulent magnetar fields follows, then Galileo's reprogrammable navigation module, and a supercomputer rover bound for the lunar south pole. Japan's Fugaku Supercomputer ran high-resolution models of low-mass neutron-star binaries, according to Fizz.org.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:34.480
Though stars started with magnetic fields around 3 trillion gauze, already in the upper

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.620
range for ordinary neutron stars, the study is posted on the archive under the title,

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.960
a magnetar formation in binary neutron star merger.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:49.280
Most neutron stars sit above the Chandrasakar limit that caps white dwarfs, yet remain below

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.660
the Tolman Oppenheimer Volkhoff limit near 2.6 solar masses.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.880
That mass window lets some mergers leave a single neutron star instead of a black hole.

00:00:59.799 --> 00:01:04.159
The calculation opened just before the stars touched and stopped shortly after contact,

00:01:04.679 --> 00:01:07.419
while the mixed fluid still had time to change the remnant.

00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:13.739
Imagine two spheres the size of cities, each denser than an atomic nucleus, spiraling in at

00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:15.339
hundreds of kilometers per second.

00:01:15.919 --> 00:01:18.139
Contact does not flatten them into one lump.

00:01:18.699 --> 00:01:22.079
The interface shears the way two rivers smash together at right angles.

00:01:22.079 --> 00:01:27.999
The shear sets off Kelvin Helmholtz instability, those rolling billows you get when wind lifts

00:01:27.999 --> 00:01:28.699
candle smoke.

00:01:29.399 --> 00:01:33.899
Inside the neutron fluid, the rolls stretch and fold embedded magnetic field lines the

00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:38.699
way Taffy is pulled, packing more lines into the same volume and driving the field up.

00:01:39.319 --> 00:01:43.459
That folding finished soon enough for the new field to steer the merger's later evolution

00:01:43.459 --> 00:01:44.959
before the remnant could settle.

00:01:45.639 --> 00:01:49.719
Fields like these already sit thousands of times stronger than anything built on Earth.

00:01:50.279 --> 00:01:51.419
The gain is not even.

00:01:51.419 --> 00:01:56.419
It starts in a thin turbulent sheet and then spreads as the mixed fluid circulates outward.

00:01:56.959 --> 00:02:04.219
The burst is fast enough to alter internal pressure support, and it may decide whether the object stays intact or collapses further.

00:02:04.859 --> 00:02:12.359
The runs treated the two stars as equal in mass and covered the stretch from just before contact until the instability had finished its first work.

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:17.579
How far that turbulence can shove the field before it saturates or reconnects is still open?

00:02:18.259 --> 00:02:21.199
The calculation halted once the first mixing burst ended.

00:02:21.419 --> 00:02:26.619
Longer runs, or different mass ratios, might show extra gain or a field that starts

00:02:26.619 --> 00:02:27.839
to resist more stretching.

00:02:28.479 --> 00:02:33.659
No telescope can yet read the internal field of a freshly merged neutron star, so these

00:02:33.659 --> 00:02:38.019
models are the clearest forecasts we have for how at least some magnetars are born.

00:02:38.679 --> 00:02:43.599
The same folding still operates when the starting fields are already intense, which hints the

00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:47.079
process is common among mergers that leave a neutron star behind.

00:02:47.079 --> 00:02:52.719
Runs that continue past that first millisecond are needed to test whether the field keeps climbing,

00:02:53.279 --> 00:02:58.959
or whether reconnection caps the gain. The models show the roles forming, yet no one knows exactly

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:05.099
how the amplified field redistributes angular momentum inside the remnant, or whether it changes the odds of

00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:12.563
a later collapse to a black hole. The European Space Agency, or ESA, has already tested a reprogrammable

00:03:12.563 --> 00:03:16.163
navigation module in to lose for Galileo second generation.

00:03:16.783 --> 00:03:21.983
The box will generate multiple navigation signals in real time and take in-orbit updates

00:03:21.983 --> 00:03:23.143
that create new ones.

00:03:23.683 --> 00:03:28.543
It is due to pair with the radio frequency output module next year before both units

00:03:28.543 --> 00:03:30.103
go into the first flight satellite.

00:03:30.683 --> 00:03:35.923
Twelve second generation satellites will join the constellation, each flying electric propulsion,

00:03:36.523 --> 00:03:39.643
inter-satellite links, and an enhanced navigation antenna.

00:03:39.643 --> 00:03:45.163
Airbus Defense and Space has six of those satellites at various integration stages in

00:03:45.163 --> 00:03:50.403
its Friedrichshoff and Hall, and the navigation antennas recently finished evaluation in Madrid.

00:03:51.023 --> 00:03:56.223
The module reads timing data from advanced atomic clocks and can harden the signals against

00:03:56.223 --> 00:03:58.543
interference or tune them for faster lock.

00:03:59.731 --> 00:04:05.091
Astrolab's Flex Lunar Innovation Platform carries a supercomputer and will ride as a payload

00:04:05.091 --> 00:04:11.031
on Voyager Technology's Griffin Mission 1 in late 2026, according to Space.com.

00:04:11.411 --> 00:04:19.911
The rover will work at the lunar South Pole and test tires, batteries, radios, and cameras identical to those planned for the crewed lunar terrain vehicle.

00:04:20.551 --> 00:04:25.671
Its data will feed the design of Astrolab CLV-1 before crewed Artemis landings.

00:04:26.211 --> 00:04:34.051
Viper, the South Polar Rover from the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA, is fully assembled at Johnson Space Center.

00:04:34.051 --> 00:04:39.651
After a 2027 landing, it will travel more than 18 miles across permanently shadowed

00:04:39.651 --> 00:04:40.091
craters.

00:04:40.631 --> 00:04:45.971
Astrolab engineers will soon put CLV-1's airless wheels through NASA Glenn's simulated

00:04:45.971 --> 00:04:47.491
lunar operations laboratory.

00:04:48.537 --> 00:04:53.777
Tower arms snag a starship upper stage in a new SpaceX animation reported by Tesla

00:04:53.777 --> 00:04:54.177
Roddy.

00:04:54.537 --> 00:04:56.837
The sequence shows the booster landing first.

00:04:57.517 --> 00:05:00.377
Hours later, the ship comes in once the tower is clear.

00:05:00.377 --> 00:05:06.377
air. SpaceX is asking the Federal Aviation Administration, or FAA, to approve a double

00:05:06.377 --> 00:05:11.057
catch attempt planned for next month. The clip shows the ship approaching the tower from

00:05:11.057 --> 00:05:16.777
above after the booster is already locked in. A neutron star spinning more than 300 times

00:05:16.777 --> 00:05:22.757
per second appeared to break far too fast, and Fizz.org reports that the extra drag unmasked

00:05:22.757 --> 00:05:27.997
two companion stars. The layout is the first known pulsar in a hierarchical triple at

00:05:27.997 --> 00:05:32.857
this spin-rate. The apparent slowdown comes from the pulsar's motion around a shared center

00:05:32.857 --> 00:05:38.257
of mass, which periodically shifts the pulse arrival time seen from Earth. More timing

00:05:38.257 --> 00:05:43.885
should pin down the companion's masses and their orbits. Researchers at Columbia University

00:05:43.885 --> 00:05:49.685
and Imperial College London mapped young fault scarps inside Valas Marineris, the solar

00:05:49.685 --> 00:05:55.785
system's largest canyon, according to Fizz.org. The scarps point to ongoing tectonic extension

00:05:55.785 --> 00:06:01.085
driven by a cooling interior. That motion is still running billions of years after the canyon

00:06:01.085 --> 00:06:06.745
first opened. The shrinkage rate stays small, yet it is enough to set off occasional landslides

00:06:06.745 --> 00:06:14.520
on the steep walls. NGC 7129 shows proto-stellar jets in new images from the James Webb Space Telescope,

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:22.660
or JWST, released by NASA. Golden patches trace hot atomic hydrogen, and red patches mark cooler

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:28.400
molecular hydrogen shocked by the outflows. Embedded protostars drive those jets. The

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:33.240
flows stir and heat the cloud around them. The frame holds bright atomic hydrogen in the

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.620
core and a wider shroud of molecular hydrogen hit by the jets.

00:06:37.913 --> 00:06:42.853
Formaldehyde rained onto early Mars billions of years ago and a new model tracks how it could

00:06:42.853 --> 00:06:49.013
have concentrated in surface minerals, universe-today reports. The map flags regions where prebiotic

00:06:49.013 --> 00:06:50.533
chemistry may have run hottest.

00:06:51.033 --> 00:06:55.273
Future landers could aim at those sites in a search for chemical precursors to life.

00:06:55.713 --> 00:07:00.373
The work follows how that early precipitation carried and laid down the key prebiotic

00:07:00.373 --> 00:07:00.933
molecule.

00:07:01.945 --> 00:07:07.005
A flexible patch from KIST releases medication straight onto the eye without any help

00:07:07.005 --> 00:07:09.585
from gravity, according to Fizz.org.

00:07:10.045 --> 00:07:15.545
The device was built with Seoul National University Bundang Hospital and published in sensors and

00:07:15.545 --> 00:07:16.945
actuators B, chemical.

00:07:16.945 --> 00:07:21.325
It targets a practical barrier for crews on long-duration spaceflight.

00:07:21.705 --> 00:07:26.305
The design keeps the dose steady even when surface tension and a lack of sedimentation

00:07:26.305 --> 00:07:27.785
control how fluid behaves.

00:07:28.839 --> 00:07:34.699
Osiris Apex is still flying toward asteroid Apophis after bringing Bennu Samples home, according

00:07:34.699 --> 00:07:35.779
